inventories.assets:
Checked-in, not script-generated: apps/simulator-macos/Resources/Dirt/
- diffuse.jpg: 1024x1024 RGB 8-bit, 730 KB.
- normal.jpg: 1024x1024 RGB, 1.2 MB, OpenGL (+Y) convention per ATTRIBUTION.md.
- roughness.jpg: 1024x1024 8-bit greyscale, 254 KB.
Source: Poly Haven "Dirt" by Charlotte Baglioni (https://polyhaven.com/a/dirt), CC0 1.0, downloaded as 1K JPEGs from api.polyhaven.com/files/dirt and MD5-verified. There are no licensing constraints. Poly Haven also offers 2K/4K/8K and a DirectX normal variant, which helps D3D-convention engines, though "same graphics" means keeping the 1K maps. build-app.sh:47 copies the folder into the app bundle; the textures are loaded by DirtWorld.swift:88-91. If they are missing, a procedural fallback is used.

Brick walls also use the city plaster scans (Resources/City/plaster-base.jpg, plaster-normal.jpg and plaster-rough.jpg, covered by the City reader).

Everything else in this subsystem is generated at runtime on the CPU with deterministic integer hashes, so the outputs are reproducible and could be pre-baked offline for a port:
- packed earth 512² RGBA8, generated twice;
- soil 1024x256 RGBA8, up to 3 variants;
- infield deposit 768² RGBA8 premultiplied;
- dust plume and haze 128² RGBA8;
- plaster wear atlas 1024² (TownCrowd.swift);
- sky probe 64x32 RGBA8, regenerated on every sky apply;
- sand height textures R32F 67x67 (dynamic);
- coating state RGBA32F 2x1 (dynamic).

All meshes (track, walls, aprons, 560 dune tiles x 3 LODs, drifts, belts) are procedural from SimulationCore maths. There are no .scn, .dae or glTF assets for this subsystem, and no Python script in /Users/m1/marvin/scripts touches Resources/Dirt; prepare-simulator-assets.py and export-*.py are for robots and the city. Licensing for procedural content: project-owned.

inventories.overview:
SimulationCore (20 files, 2,333 lines, 130.8 KB) is a self-contained, almost entirely platform-neutral Swift game-logic library. It has no rendering or UI code. Its parts:
- Simulation (Simulation.swift) is a value-type differential-drive robot. In the sandbox it is kinematic with oriented-footprint obstacle checks. On the dirt track it adds terrain following, ballistic jumps and, when DirtRacePhysics enables robotDynamics, momentum, finite traction, storm drag and a yaw-torque limit. It also handles head yaw/pitch with CAD clearance boxes, track/wheel travel and checkpoints.
- DirtCourse (DirtRace.swift) defines the closed 16-point periodic cubic B-spline circuit: 768 samples, a bounding-volume tree for projection, an analytic elevation/bank/berm/service-ramp height field, and fence projection.
- DirtRace handles countdown, laps and the cooldown lap. DirtScores is a JSON top-10 leaderboard. DirtStandings orders racers.
- DirtRacePhysics is the 240 Hz fixed-step accumulator for all four racers. Each substep advances storm, wind shelter, post-race escape, gate, assists or AI input, each Simulation, the RobotCollisions sequential-impulse solver (24 iterations, plus fence, ground, infield, gate and town solids), CollisionRecovery, and then lap progress.
- DirtOpponent is the AI driver (120 Hz when run standalone). DirtDrivingAssists/DirtRacingLine provide predictive braking and blended steering.
- Sandstorm handles wind, drifts and drag. DesertTerrain is a 769×769 baked dune heightfield. SandDeformation is a 64-tile LRU sand heightfield made of 4 m × 64×64 Float tiles.
- Course entry and exit: CityExit/CityGate (motorised hinge gate), CityCollisionWorld (8 m spatial buckets over about 6,400 town solids), InfieldLayout (seeded salvage and tent fixtures), TownEscapeRoute (grid BFS route planning with line-of-sight simplification and Bézier rounding) and PostRaceEscape (four robots leave through the gate and roam town indefinitely).
- Presentation helpers: RacePerformance (character acting and attention poses), RaceMapRegion/OverviewMotion/TownFootprint (map context and critically damped overview camera), and TrackLoop/HeadRig/HeadClearance (tread belt, neck kinematics, head-to-body separating-axis checks).

All physics is Double precision except the SandDeformation tile storage (Float). The only Apple-only dependency is `import simd` in 13 of the 20 files: about 70 call sites of simd_dot/length/length_squared/distance/normalize/cross plus one simd_quatd/simd_double3x3/.act use. Foundation is used for libm math, and for JSON, files and Date in DirtScores.

The tests (15 files, 1,671 lines) are not XCTest. They are an executable target, SimulationChecks, with an @main runner calling 39 check functions. These use require/near helpers, fputs and exit(1), and take the flags --dunes-only, --escape-only and --assists-only. They check physics invariants, frame-rate independence (10/30/60/120 fps), energy and momentum, geometry seams, and render/collision agreement for the Metal sand snapshot and terrain triangulation. CI (ubuntu) does not run them.

Substantial game logic still lives in the app target, AppController in App.swift:
- the session and race controller: intro/countdown/outro timers, grid shuffle, a 10% storm draw, random daylight, score recording, and pause/focus/occlusion gating;
- the game loop: a 60 Hz Timer with wall-clock dt clamped to 0.1 s for physics, while the race clock uses the unclamped wall delta;
- all camera logic: follow/orbit/overview, intro/outro smootherstep flights, boom collision against town solids, and a locked post-race overview;
- input mapping (SimulatorView) and the voice director (RaceVoiceDirector);
- daylight sun geometry (BinaryDaylight);
- the town resident and pedestrian AI (TownResidents, TownStreetResidents, TownAccessMap), which uses SCNNode.isHidden and frustum visibility as state;
- dust and clod particle simulation (DirtWorld; positions stored in SCNNode), tread-mark spacing, dirt-coating accumulation, and the 30 Hz sand-deformation tick, which runs from the render update rather than the physics clock;
- per-robot model kinematics (wheel angles, BB-8 shell quaternion integration, WALL-E belt links);
- the deterministic procedural town generator (TownWorld, seeded LCG 0xA57E2026). It emits the roughly 6,400 collision solids, doorways and walking routes that core physics and PostRaceEscape depend on, as a side effect of building meshes.

inventories.assets:
SimulationCore uses no files or binary assets. All of its data is generated procedurally at static init:
- DesertTerrain: 591,361 Double samples (4.73 MB) from an integer-hash noise;
- DirtCourse: 769 spline samples and a projection tree;
- DirtRacingLine: 768 points after 240 smoothing passes;
- InfieldLayout: 16 salvage parts from LCG seed 0x5a17cafe over 900 candidates, giving about 60 obstacle bodies and 2 canopies;
- Sandstorm: 17 drifts; CityExit statics; RaceMapRegion: a 769-point enclosure.

These are deterministic and must be reproduced bit-for-bit (same hash/LCG arithmetic) to keep the same dunes and salvage placement. CourseLayout uses SplitMix64 with the Swift stdlib's Double.random(in:using:) and shuffle(using:) algorithms.

Physics data flows in from app-side assets:
- BB-8 and WALL-E sand contact footprints come from Resources/{BB8,WallE}/Generated/mesh.json ('contacts', 'height', 'ballRadius', 'beltLength'), produced by scripts/export-racers-simulator.py and racer_gltf.py;
- R2-D2 contacts are hard-coded (R2D2.swift:16-18); Marvin's contacts are 0.262225·modelScale, where modelScale = R2D2.sceneHeight·(0.60/1.08)/neutralHeight comes from the STEP-derived mesh (scripts/export-marvin-simulator.py).

Town collision solids (about 6,400), doorways, walking streets, spectator and town sound zones, and camera bounds are generated at load time by TownWorld (seeded LCG 0xA57E2026) together with render meshes and the crowd asset. They are not stored as data today; exporting them to versioned JSON would decouple the port.

Persistent user data: the scores JSON array [{date: Double (s since 2001-01-01), laps: [Double]×3}], top 10, at ~/Library/Application Support/Marvin Simulator/motocross-v4-scores.json; plus six UserDefaults booleans.

Licensing: core logic is first-party code with no third-party dependencies. The CC BY 4.0 model attributions (R2-D2 LordDiego, BB-8 Willy Decarpentrie, WALL-E Janis Zeps) and CERN-OHL-S-2.0 hardware geometry only matter here because contact footprints are derived from those meshes.

inventories.quantitative:
Code size:
- SimulationCore: 20 files, 2,333 lines, 130,790 bytes; 13 files import simd.
- Tests: 15 files, 1,671 lines, 93,790 bytes; 39 check functions, all invoked by the @main CheckRunner; SimulationChecks builds in 4.92 s (release, M2).
- App target: 70 files, 12,902 lines, 830,701 bytes. Largest: TownWorld 2,494 lines, App 1,159, DirtWorld 765. About 30 *Smoke.swift files plus App.swift's smoke section (lines 229-418 and 787-1158).

Simulation load: 4 racers × 240 substeps/s = 960 robot integrations/s. The solver handles up to 24 iterations per substep over 6 robot pairs plus, per body, about 60 infield obstacles, 2 canopies, gate and posts, and nearby town solids from the 6,400-solid index with 8 m buckets.
- Projection tree: 768 segments, leaves ≤12 segments. About 10-15 projections per robot per substep (traction ×2, course speed, constraint ×2, recovery, race ×2, assists/AI), so roughly 10-15k projections/s.
- Wind shelter probes: 3 per robot every 24 substeps (10 Hz).
- Sand deformation: up to 64 tiles × (4,096 Float delta + 4,096 Float base) ≈ 2 MB; ticked at 30 Hz; GPU snapshot 67×67 = 4,489 floats per dirty tile.
- Desert terrain: 769² samples (4.73 MB).
- Particles: pool 1,600, 2 batched draws.
- Trails: up to 128 chunks × 256 quads = 32,768 marks per racer (4 racers).
- Residents: ≤10 door walkers and ≤24 street walkers. One recorded run made 83,169 navigation updates against 360,000 full-rate updates (77% fewer).
- Storm visuals: 960 particles.

Timing on Apple M2 at 1920×1080, from the ten-minute-drive summary.json (603 s, timer driver): CPU game update p95 was 2.47-2.90 ms per minute bucket. This covers physics, models, effects, camera and town; physics alone is not broken out. Mean render 57.2 fps; worst frame 52.3 ms. Control runs in dirt-city.md show game update p95 of 2.58-3.71 ms.

Recorded SimulationChecks output (docs/audio-validation/2026-10-02-round2/simulation-checks.txt):
- weather draws 1006/10000;
- drift mesh/collision error 0.004015 m;
- dune peak 9.111 m, maximum slope 32.89°;
- sand cut -0.03486 m and bank 0.01939 m; 64 tiles, 324 allocations;
- coupled race totals 91.537 / 101.491 / 90.334 s at 30/60/120 fps, with 800 / 3,066 / 2,292 contacts (the test limit on the spread is <12 s);
- R2-D2 three-lap total 78.26190110394832 s, identical at 30/60/120 fps;
- missed-braking contacts drop from 170 to 98 with assists;
- exact projection check: 24,499 queries;
- lap length 136.25642662849137;
- city exit centre (18.9809, -8.9962), sill 0.43636.

Frame-rate independence is tested at:
- 10/30/60/120 fps for coupled physics (1e-7 tolerance);
- 30/60/120 fps for storm (1e-5), assists (1e-8), acting (1e-8), overview camera (1e-9) and AI finish time (0.001 s);
- 30 vs 120 fps for sandbox kinematics (1e-4).

Model triangles (README): Marvin 695,172 (23 parts); R2-D2 25,158; BB-8 7,198; WALL-E 40,158.
